= Conjunction fallacy =

== Overview ==
The '''conjunction fallacy''' occurs when a conjunction of events is judged more probable than one of its constituent events, contrary to the conjunction rule of probability.

== Classic paradigm ==
The Linda problem showed that a detailed description could make a conjunction appear more representative than a broader constituent category.

== Interpretation ==
The effect has been explained through representativeness, natural-language interpretation, conversational pragmatics, and task representation. Experimental design therefore matters.

== References ==
* Amos Tversky & Daniel Kahneman, “Extensional versus Intuitive Reasoning: The Conjunction Fallacy in Probability Judgment”, ''Psychological Review'' 90 (1983): 293–315.
* subsequent conjunction-fallacy and frequency-format literature.

